Unexplained change in bowel habits, Rectal bleeding/iron deficiency anaemia, Unintentional weight loss, Irregular friable mass on colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-016
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of colicky abdominal pain and repeated vomiting in a patient with recent weight loss, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute mechanical bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a progressive 48-hour history of severe, cramping lower abdominal pain culminating tonight in repeated, large-volume bilious vomiting and an inability to pass flatus. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate for acute malignant bowel obstruction, which poses an immediate risk of bowel ischemia or perforation if not diagnosed prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has been cramping so badly it doubles me over, I've thrown up four times tonight, and I haven't passed any gas since yesterday."
History of Present Illness: The 68-year-old patient reports an insidious 4-month history of a progressive change in normal bowel habits, characterized by alternating severe constipation and new-onset persistent diarrhoea. They have noted visible dark, tarry melaena intermittently over the last month and a 6kg unintentional significant weight loss. Tonight, they experienced a sharp escalation of severe, progressive colicky abdominal pain accompanied by significant nausea and recurrent vomiting. They report profound anorexia.
Functional Status: Bedbound tonight due to severe pain and exhaustion from vomiting.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid dehydration and electrolyte derangement overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Pale, visibly distressed adult, lying supine with knees drawn up.
Physical Exam:
Abdomen: Visibly distended. Auscultation reveals high-pitched, "tinkling" bowel sounds initially, followed by periods of complete silence. Palpation reveals a firm, fixed abdominal mass in the left lower quadrant. Diffuse tenderness is present, but no rigid board-like peritonitis or rebound tenderness.
Gastrointestinal: Digital rectal exam reveals an empty rectal vault; stool on the gloved finger is strongly positive for dark, altered blood.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, capillary refill 3 seconds, dry oral mucosa indicating moderate dehydration.
Skin: Marked pallor of the conjunctivae, suggesting underlying iron deficiency anaemia.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin containing bilious fluid next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent opioid use or new constipating agents.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Colorectal Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Mechanical Bowel Obstruction)
Differentials:

Severe Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the insidious history of significant weight loss, palpable fixed mass, and classic obstructive presentation rather than acute localized inflammatory pain with high fever.

Ischemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the chronic, progressive change in bowel habits over months rather than an abrupt, agonizing event out of proportion to physical findings.

Severe Constipation (Faecal Impaction) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the empty rectal vault on DRE, left lower quadrant fixed mass, and profound systemic weight loss/anorexia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic tetrad of malignant large bowel obstruction: colicky pain, vomiting, abdominal distension, and absolute constipation (lack of flatus). The preceding history of melaena, weight loss, and a palpable left lower quadrant mass points overwhelmingly to an advanced colorectal malignancy. Delaying assessment until the morning hours would expose the patient to uncontrolled pain, severe hypovolemia, and the critical risk of closed-loop bowel perforation and fatal peritonitis. This acute surgical emergency definitively mandates an urgent after-hours home assessment to diagnose the obstruction and initiate immediate hospital transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance for urgent surgical review, abdominal CT scan (anticipating a transition point and potential irregular friable mass on eventual colonoscopy), and intravenous fluid resuscitation/nasogastric decompression.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Maintain strict "nil by mouth" (NPO) status tonight. By completely stopping all food and liquid intake, you prevent any further fluid buildup above the blockage in your bowel, reducing the risk of vomiting and perforation.

Remain strictly in bed until the paramedics arrive. By resting, you decrease your body's oxygen demand and reduce the risk of fainting from dehydration.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

